  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quasi stationary high pres sprawling across the\nMid- Atlantic and low pressure well off the FL coast will \nresult in cont northeasterly flow across the region today. The \nhigh waves combined with King Tides this week will lead to \nadditional minor coastal flooding impacts on the OBX.\n\nMinor coastal flooding of vulnerable locations will be likely \nwell into this week despite the diminishing of winds, with \n\"sunny day\" flooding across much of the tidal regions of \ncoastal ENC around times of high tide, then slowly improve as \nthe King Tides ease later in the week and into the weekend. \nCoastal flood advisories continue through the high tide cycle on\nThursday with the potential to be extended further in time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quasi stationary dry high pres cont to sprawl \nacross the region with weak coastal troughing offshore. Some\nmoisture has pushed inland today bringing a few sprinkles or\nlight rain showers to locations south of highway 70, as well\nas portions of the OBX. For the latter half of the week into \nthe weekend, hts/thicknesses build further, and temps climbing \nback to ~10 degrees above climo with highs upr 70s to near 80 \ninland to low 70s OBX. Low temps will warm above climo as well \nwith readings in the mid 50s for most locales away from the \nimmediate coast. \n\nHigh pres ridge may begin to break down as early as Sunday, but\nrecent runs from EC/AIFS cont to keep a stronger high in place.\nMay have to wait until a stronger srn stream system sweeps\nacross the Deep South and into the region later next week to \nsee next good chance for appreciable rain. For now, pops remain \nquite low for late Sun into Mon ~30% to account for the small \nshower chances.",
